<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Honduras</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Honduras</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Honduras is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. Before Spanish colonisation in the 16th century its territory held several Mesoamerican cultures, most notably the Maya, whose state at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copan">Copán</a> declined in the 9th century. Honduras became independent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1821, was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a> until 1823, and then of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">United Provinces of Central America</a>. It has been an independent republic with elections since 1838. In the early 20th century transnational agricultural companies dominated it, and the term &quot;banana republic&quot; was first coined for its politics. In 1960 the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquito Coast</a> was transferred from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> to Honduras.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/honduras.jpg" alt="Honduras" width="330" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kes47 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Honduras_(1949–2022,_2026–present).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Honduras covers about 112,492 square kilometres. It is bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> to the southwest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> to the southeast, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> to the south at the Gulf of Fonseca and the Gulf of Honduras, an inlet of the Caribbean Sea, to the north. The name means depths in Spanish, and may refer to the bay of Trujillo as an anchorage or to a remark attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>. The capital and largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the Spanish conquest the territory was split between two cultural regions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> in the west and the Isthmo-Colombian area in the east, each with a core area in Honduras: the far west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mosquitia">La Mosquitia</a>. Columbus landed near Trujillo in 1502 on his fourth voyage and became the first European to visit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-islands">Bay Islands</a>. His brother Bartholomew met a Mayan trading vessel from Yucatán there, in the first recorded encounter between the Spanish and the Maya. Honduras was organised as a province of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, with its capital first at Trujillo, later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comayagua">Comayagua</a> and finally at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a>.</p><p>Honduras became independent in 1821, belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a> until 1823 and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">United Provinces of Central America</a>. It has been a republic with regular elections since 1838. In the late 19th century it granted land and tax exemptions to US-based fruit and infrastructure companies. Banana companies built an enclave economy in the north that contributed relatively little to growth, and American troops landed in Honduras in 1903, 1907, 1911, 1912, 1919, 1924 and 1925. The term banana republic was first coined for this corporate-dominated politics.</p><p>In 1969 Honduras and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/football-war">Football War</a>, after border tensions and the Honduran president's blaming of Salvadoran immigrants for the economy. The Salvadoran army invaded on 14 July, a ceasefire negotiated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organization-of-american-states">Organization of American States</a> took effect on 20 July, and as many as 130,000 Salvadoran immigrants were expelled. A military coup overthrew the civilian government in 1963, and civilian rule returned in 1979. A coup in 2009 removed President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-zelaya">Manuel Zelaya</a> from office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Honduras is a presidential republic whose National Congress has 128 members elected for four years. Society is predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">Mestizo</a>, with Indigenous, Black and White communities. The Spanish brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-language">Spanish language</a>, which blended with Indigenous customs. In 1960 the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquito Coast</a> was transferred from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> to Honduras by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a>. Its economy is primarily agricultural, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, tropical fruit and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane among its products.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Honduras” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It joined five states, Guatemala, El Salvador, <b>Honduras</b>, Nicaragua and Costa Rica, under a federal government seated in Guatemala City.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Diego de Landa, one son of the Cocom ruler survived the massacre of his family at Mayapan only because he was away trading in <b>Honduras</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Most Salvadorans were landless or land-poor labourers and smallholders, and population pressure drove large-scale migration to <b>Honduras</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Carrera’s armies intervened repeatedly in El Salvador and <b>Honduras</b>, defeating the liberal coalition at La Arada in 1851 and taking San Salvador in 1863, and Guatemalan contingents joined the Central American war against William Walker in 1856–1857.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1911 the banana magnate Samuel Zemurray financed an invasion of <b>Honduras</b> that restored the former president Manuel Bonilla, who rewarded him with land concessions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Today it is held by the extant Maya peoples of Guatemala, Belize, western <b>Honduras</b> and the Mexican states of Tabasco, Chiapas, Quintana Roo, Campeche and Yucatán.</blockquote><a href="/religion/maya-religion">Maya religion · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
